          ],
          "high": 8,
          "low": 2,
          "param": "m"
        }
      ],
      "why": "pipelined_m registers every m stages, so it uses fewer flip-flops and less area than full pipelining for the same accuracy, while still delivering 1 result/cycle (throughput = Fmax, easily >= 1 MSPS). Since area is the primary objective and the throughput constraint is loose, pipelined_m with larger m is the natural area-reduction lever. I keep the same accuracy-relevant ranges as pipelined and add m in 2..8 to let the optimizer trade register count (area) against the slightly longer latency (ceil(N/m)+2, which is not a constraint here). This family should dominate the low-area end of the front."
    }
  ],
  "rationale": "The spec is area-minimum with a loose throughput floor (>=1 MSPS) and a hard accuracy bound (max_abs_err <= 2^-10). Both of my families are 1-result/cycle, so throughput is simply Fmax, which is trivially satisfied at motor-control clock rates; the real trade-off is area vs accuracy_bits. Accuracy is identical across families for the same (data_width, n_iter, angle_guard, frac_guard, rounding), so I keep those ranges aligned and let n_iter (10..18) and data_width (12..28) span the accuracy/area curve, with the small guards as fine knobs. I split the budget 60/40 between pipelined (baseline, full register set) and pipelined_m (register-every-m, the main area-reduction mechanism). This covers the whole feasible trade-off curve: pipelined_m should claim the minimum-area corner (the selection point), while pipelined and higher n_iter/data_width points extend the front toward higher accuracy_bits, maximizing hypervolume against the reference (area 40000, accuracy 10)."
}

